In the context of building a component registry with Angular, you can utilize the `defineRegistry` function to map each catalog component to its actual rendering implementation. This allows for type-safe bindings between the catalog properties and your component functions. Here's a basic example of how you might set up a component registry in Angular:

```typescript
import { Component } from "@angular/core";
import { injectStream } from "@langchain/angular";
import { AIMessage, HumanMessage } from "langchain";

@Component({
  selector: "app-chat",
  template: `
    <ng-container *ngFor="let msg of stream.messages(); trackBy: trackByFn">
      <ng-container *ngIf="isHuman(msg)">
        <human-bubble [text]="msg.text"></human-bubble>
      </ng-container>
      <ng-container *ngIf="isAI(msg)">
        <ai-response
          [message]="msg"
          [isStreaming]="stream.isLoading() && $index === stream.messages().length - 1"
        ></ai-response>
      </ng-container>
    </ng-container>
  `,
})
export class ChatComponent {
  stream = injectStream({
    apiUrl: "http://localhost:2024",
    assistantId: "reasoning",
  });

  isHuman = HumanMessage.isInstance;
  isAI = AIMessage.isInstance;

  trackByFn(index: number, item: any) {
    return item.id; // or any unique identifier
  }
}
```

In this example, the `ChatComponent` uses the `injectStream` function to connect to a stream of messages. It iterates over the messages and conditionally renders different components based on whether the message is from a human or an AI. This setup allows for a structured way to manage and render components dynamically based on the data received.

